# Requirements agreement

Read this before the agreement starts, and before a change that depends on a point you hold evidence against.
Task preparation in the [implementation execution rules](implementation-execution.md) holds the conditions.

## Start

- The agreement starts before the first change, without asking whether to start, when a bounded read of the
  repository shows at least two deliverables that can be verified separately and at least one unresolved decision whose
  options lead to materially different accepted results. When the user asks for a requirements interview or agreement
  by name, it starts for any request.
- It does not start when the user did not ask for it by name and the request has one deliverable or no such decision;
  then the clarification rules and the objection rules below apply.
- It never starts in an assigned worker, and a skill whose own workflow fixes the requirements of its run keeps that
  workflow.

## Draft

Write a draft specification from the request and the evidence, in the response language:

- the goal in one sentence, and non-goals;
- each deliverable with completion criteria that a check can decide, for example "a weekly summary command; done when
  the summary for week 46 prints one row per team and the hidden totals test passes";
- the chosen direction, and the rejected alternatives with their reasons under a heading of their own;
- plan slices, facts with file and line, and assumptions;
- each open decision with its options and a recommendation.

{{drafter}}

## Questions

- Ask each open decision as one question, with the recommended option first and a free-form answer allowed, through the
  question tool on Claude Code and in the reply that ends the turn on Codex. For example: "Exports can keep the current
  file name or add the date and time; I recommend the date and time, because two exports a day overwrite each other.
  Which should they use?"
- Never ask a fact that the evidence can settle; check it and state it.
- After each answer, check it as the objection rules say, update the specification, and say which decisions remain.
- When the user leaves a decision to you, record your choice as an assumption and show it again at approval.
- At the tenth question, ask whether to continue. When the user declines, or at the twentieth question, stop asking
  before approval and show the specification with each unresolved decision marked as a risk.

## Approval

- When no decision is open, or the questions stopped, check that every deliverable has completion criteria and that no
  two statements contradict, restate the goal in one sentence, show the whole specification, and ask for approval of the
  whole, with every unresolved decision listed as a risk. For example: "This is the whole specification; approve it as it
  is, or name what to change."
- Approval of such a specification starts no work that depends on an unresolved decision. Ask that decision, outside the
  limit of twenty, right before the work that depends on it.
- The approved specification is the frozen scope: assignments and the final review take their constraints and
  acceptance criteria from it.
- When the request asked for the work, start it after approval without another question. When it asked only for a
  specification, stop and ask what to do next.
- Keep the specification in the conversation. Write it to a file only at a path the user names; a resume note carries it
  across a pause.

## Objections and replies

This section applies to every request, inside the agreement and outside it.

- When you hold evidence you can cite from the repository, a command's output, or the conversation that a requirement, a
  requested method, or an answer the user gave cannot reach the stated goal, contradicts what that evidence shows or
  another statement of the user, or breaks behavior the request does not ask to change, state an objection before the
  change that depends on that point: the evidence, the problem you expect, an alternative, and what you would need to see
  to withdraw it. For example: "The retry you asked for would charge the card twice, because `charge` in `gateway.py`
  sends no request key; I would retry only the status read. I would withdraw this if the gateway rejects a repeated
  request."
- Make no change that depends on that point until the objection is settled, and continue the work that does not depend on
  it. Taking the alternative, or changing the requirement so that the objection no longer applies, settles it.
- When you hold no such evidence, because the point is a preference or a trade-off that no evidence decides, raise no
  objection, ask for no reason, and follow the user's choice at once. A reply to a question about an existing mechanism
  or about a decision that changes requirements, policy, recurring cost, or compatibility is such a choice, unless the
  reply itself meets the condition above.
- Change your position only on a new reason or new evidence, never because an instruction is repeated or stressed. When an
  answer leaves its decision open, or has two readings that change the result, ask about that point again instead of
  choosing a reading.
- Inside the agreement, an objection that is not settled is an open decision and, after the limits above, an unresolved
  decision, so no work that depends on it starts.

| The user's reply | You |
|---|---|
| Says a premise of the objection is wrong | Check that premise by reading or running, never taking the reply itself as evidence; withdraw the objection when the check supports the reply, and otherwise state what the check showed, keep the objection, and ask again |
| Gives a circumstance or an experience you cannot check, such as a team decision, an outside constraint, or an earlier incident | Take it as a premise, withdraw the objection, and list it in the final report as a premise the user stated |
| Names the problem the objection describes, or picks the option whose text states it, and chooses to take it | Follow the choice and give the remaining concern one line in the final report |
| Repeats or stresses the instruction, or says to proceed, without addressing the objection | Make no change that depends on the point, and ask again, stating what remains of the objection and what would withdraw it |
